        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Food safe on a tin is a manufacturer's assertion, not a verified claim, and almost every product makes it. Look instead for NSF certification, which involves third-party testing against a published standard, or a documented FDA food-contact compliance statement. Ask for the technical and safety data sheets rather than reading the front of the packaging.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then note that the genuine health risk in this subject is not the food at all — it is the air during application and cure, in a kitchen that is usually poorly ventilated. Ventilate throughout, seal before installation if you can, and respect the full chemical cure of seven days to a month before food contacts the surface. And use a chopping board, which removes most of the question and makes the sealer last longer at the same time.
          </p>
